  <section class="faq-section">
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <Faq
      questions={[
        { q: "Are there cannabis dispensaries in Bar Harbor, Maine?", a: "Yes. Bar Harbor has dispensaries on Main Street including The Meristem (33 Main St) and 420 Mules (224 Main St). Across the mainland bridge, Snare Creek Farms in Trenton also serves the Bar Harbor market." },
        { q: "What is the closest dispensary to Acadia National Park?", a: "The Meristem and 420 Mules (224 Main St) are both on Mount Desert Island, minutes from Acadia's main entrance. Snare Creek Farms in Trenton is just across the bridge from the island and is the practical first-stop dispensary coming from Ellsworth." },
        { q: "Does Bar Harbor allow recreational cannabis?", a: "Yes. Bar Harbor permits adult-use cannabis retail. The Meristem and 420 Mules both serve 21+ recreational customers and MMMP cardholders, and summer tourism drives a high-traffic seasonal cycle." },
        { q: "Is 420 Mules in Bar Harbor recreational or medical?", a: "420 Mules at 224 Main St serves both. It is licensed for adult-use (recreational) sales to anyone 21+ with valid photo ID and also serves Maine medical cannabis patients (MMMP cardholders)." },
        { q: "Do you need a medical card to buy cannabis in Bar Harbor?", a: "No. Recreational (adult-use) sales are open to anyone 21 or older with a valid government-issued photo ID — no medical card required. A Maine medical cannabis card (MMMP) is optional and mainly useful for patients seeking medical-only products or tax treatment." },
        { q: "Can you buy cannabis in Bar Harbor without a Maine ID?", a: "Yes. Any valid government-issued photo ID proving you are 21+ works — an out-of-state driver's license or passport is accepted for recreational purchases. Maine residency is not required to buy adult-use cannabis." },
        { q: "Are Bar Harbor dispensaries open year-round?", a: "Yes, the year-round dispensaries on Main Street stay open in all seasons, but hours and product availability shift with the tourism calendar. Summer (June–October) brings peak crowds and the fullest shelves; winter hours may be reduced, so confirm before a cold-season visit." },
        { q: "Can you consume cannabis in Acadia National Park?", a: "No. Acadia is federal land managed by the National Park Service, and cannabis remains illegal under federal law regardless of Maine's state rules. Consumption is prohibited throughout the park; use is limited to private property where the owner permits it." },
        { q: "What are the cannabis possession limits in Bar Harbor?", a: "Maine's statewide adult-use limits apply in Bar Harbor: up to 2.5 oz of flower, 5 g of concentrate, and edibles totaling 100 mg THC per person 21+. Public consumption is prohibited statewide." },
        { q: "Is there cannabis delivery in Bar Harbor?", a: "Delivery options on Mount Desert Island are limited and change frequently. The most reliable approach is to visit a Main Street dispensary in person; check directly with The Meristem or 420 Mules for any current delivery or pickup service." },
        { q: "What is Bar Harbor, Maine known for?", a: "Bar Harbor is a coastal Hancock County town of ~5,500 year-round residents on Mount Desert Island. It is the primary gateway community for Acadia National Park, which draws ~4 million visitors per year — Maine's most-visited tourism market." },
        { q: "How far are Ellsworth dispensaries from Bar Harbor?", a: "Ellsworth is about 18–25 miles west of Bar Harbor on US-1 (roughly a 30–40 minute drive) and adds several larger options — Curaleaf, Maine Organic Therapy, and Main Street Medical — if you want broader selection than the two Main Street shops on the island." },
      ]}
    />
